<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1712">1712</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Rousseau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Jacques Rousseau was a Genevan-born philosopher, writer and composer whose works shaped the political thought of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-social-contract">The Social Contract</a> (1762) he argued that legitimate government rests on the general will of the people, and in Emile he set out a theory of education. His Confessions and novel Julie influenced the Romantic movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Rousseau" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurice Quentin de La Tour (Life time: 1788) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Quentin_de_La_Tour_-_Portrait_of_Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_-_adjusted.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Enlightenment of Montesquieu, Voltaire, <b>Rousseau</b> and Diderot's Encyclopédie was largely conducted in French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century the image of free, virtuous Alpine republics, shaped by writers such as Albrecht von Haller and the Genevan <b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>, influenced European political thought.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Plutarch's Life of Lycurgus influenced later political thinkers, including Machiavelli and <b>Rousseau</b>, and revolutionaries in eighteenth-century France cited Spartan austerity and civic virtue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…life, liberty and property and in the consent of the governed; Montesquieu urged the separation of legislative, executive and judicial power; and <b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>, whose The Social Contract opens “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains”, placed sovereignty in the people and their general will.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>’s novel Julie, or the New Heloise of 1761 made feeling and natural scenery central to literature, and Edmund Burke’s Philosophical Enquiry of 1757 distinguished the beautiful from the sublime, the thrill of awe before storms, mountains and the infinite.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Laws (1748) that it is natural for a republic to have only a small territory, and <b>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</b>, who signed himself a citizen of Geneva, held in The Social Contract (1762) that a people could make its own laws only in a state small enough for…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
